    "text": "Scientists have argued about the purpose of dreams for more than a century, and there is still no complete answer. One popular theory says that dreams help us deal [[1]] problems that we could not solve during the day. [[2]] we are asleep, the brain does not simply switch off; it keeps sorting through memories, and some of the images [[3]] appear in our dreams may be pieces of that work.\n\nAnother idea suggests that dreams let us practise facing our fears. If you have ever woken up [[4]] a pounding heart, you will know how real they can feel. On the other hand, some researchers think that dreams have no meaning [[5]] all, and are only a side effect of a busy brain. The most vivid dreams happen [[6]] REM sleep, when the eyes move quickly under the eyelids. [[7]] matter how strange a dream seems, you should not worry about it. If you sleep badly one night, a longer rest the next day can make up [[8]] some of the lost time, and your brain will probably be grateful.",
