    "text": "When my uncle announced that he was going to run the city marathon, I laughed. I had just turned eighteen, I played football twice a week, and I was convinced that I was as fit as a fiddle. “There’s nothing to it,” I told him. “You just keep running.” He gave me the kind of smile that adults keep for teenagers who are about to learn something. A week later he handed me a printed training programme and asked whether I wanted to join him. I said yes before I had time to change my mind.\n\nThe race was in April, so we began in November. At first, next spring seemed a very long way off. Then came the first long run. After eight kilometres my legs felt like wet sand, and I realised that ninety minutes of football, with its short sprints and frequent rests, had prepared me for almost nothing. My uncle, who was forty-six and looked ridiculously calm, ran beside me and said nothing. By the end of that month I seemed to live off bananas, pasta and toast, and I fell asleep during a history lesson.\n\nThe real crisis came in week six. It was dark when I got up and dark when I finished, my exams were approaching, and I could not see how I would fit in a two-hour run on Sunday as well as all my revising. I sent my uncle a message saying that I was going to drop out of the whole thing. He did not argue. He simply wrote back: “Don’t think about forty-two kilometres. Think about the next lamp post.” It was irritating advice, mainly because it worked. For the next two months, I ran from one lamp post to the next, one hill to the next, one week to the next.\n\nSlowly, the numbers began to add up. By March I had covered more than six hundred kilometres in training, and a Sunday run of twenty-eight kilometres, which would have been unthinkable in November, had become something I did before lunch. I still wasn’t sure that I could do the full distance, but I had stopped being afraid of it.\n\nOn the morning of the race, the street was full of twenty thousand runners in bright shirts, and my stomach was full of butterflies. The first half passed in a happy blur. Volunteers handed out water and slices of orange every few kilometres, and people on the pavement held up funny signs. Then, at about thirty-five kilometres, everything changed. My legs stopped obeying me, the finish line felt miles away, and for the first time since week six I wanted to quit. I slowed to a walk. A little girl on the pavement, no older than seven, held out a paper cup of jelly beans and said, “You’re nearly there!” It was not true, and we both knew it, but I took the sweets and started running again.\n\nI wrapped up the last kilometre in tears, crossing the line in four hours and forty-one minutes. My uncle was waiting for me; he had finished twenty minutes earlier. People often ask what the hardest part was, expecting me to talk about my legs. The truth is that my legs did what they had been trained to do. It was my head that needed convincing, and it needed convincing every single day.",
