    "text": "It happens to almost everyone. You hear a few seconds of a chorus in a shop, and by the evening you are still humming it while brushing your teeth, hating every note. Scientists call the phenomenon “involuntary musical imagery”; the rest of us call it an earworm. Surveys suggest that the great majority of people experience one at least once a week, which makes it one of the most common mental events that psychologists have ever tried to explain.\n\nResearchers who have collected thousands of examples have found that the songs most likely to stick are not the most beautiful or the most complicated. They tend to be fast, simple and repetitive, with a melody that rises and falls in a way that is easy to sing. But they usually contain a small surprise, such as an odd rhythm or an unexpected jump between two notes, that prevents the brain from simply filing the tune away. In other words, a song has to be predictable enough to remember and strange enough to stay interesting.\n\nRecord companies understand this very well. When a label is about to bring out a new single, its producers often test short sections of the chorus on listeners, looking for the one that people can sing back after hearing it only once. Several songwriters, speaking off the record, have admitted that they build a song around its “hook” and treat everything else as decoration. In public, however, they prefer to talk about inspiration and feelings.\n\nYet the song is only half of the story. It takes two to tango, and in this case the other dancer is the listener’s brain. Earworms rarely arrive when we are concentrating hard. They appear when our attention shuts down for a while, during a long walk, a boring chore or the minutes before sleep, and the mind, left without a task, picks up the last tune it heard and plays it again. The root cause, many psychologists believe, is not the music at all but the brain’s habit of replaying fragments of experience that it has not finished with. A tune that is cut off in the middle, for example, seems to nag us more than one we have heard right to the end.\n\nFor most people, the experience is merely annoying. For some, it is worse. Shop assistants who hear the same playlist for eight hours a day often complain that the repetition is the most tiring part of their working conditions, and a city council that plays one jingle at every bus stop may soon find that it has created hundreds of small sources of irritation. Music that is pleasant on first hearing can become a form of stress when we cannot escape from it.\n\nWhat can be done? Telling yourself to stop thinking about the tune is the surest way to keep it playing, because trying to suppress a thought tends to make it stronger. Experiments suggest that gentler methods work better: listening to the song right to the end, chewing gum, or giving the mind a moderately difficult puzzle such as an anagram or a crossword. The tune may not vanish at once, but it will usually fade within the hour. Meanwhile, if the melody of a certain chorus is already moving around your head as you read this, there is no need to be embarrassed. It is simply a sign that your brain is working as it should.",
