    "text": "When my uncle first suggested I help him keep bees on the roof of our apartment block, I assumed he was joking. Bees belonged in fields and orchards, I thought, not above a car park in the middle of the city. But Uncle Tomás is not a man who jokes about insects, and two weeks later I was standing in a borrowed white suit, feeling like a very nervous astronaut.\n\nThe first lesson, he explained, was that a hive is not a collection of insects but a single creature with thousands of bodies. \"You'll never understand them if you try to follow one bee,\" he said. \"Watch the hive.\" So I watched. For nearly an hour I sat on an upturned bucket and said nothing, while hundreds of workers streamed in and out of a gap no wider than my thumb. It was surprisingly relaxing, although my legs were numb by the end of it.\n\nLearning the ropes took most of the spring. I had imagined that the job would be all honey and no effort, but beekeeping turns out to be mainly about patience, lifting heavy boxes and checking small details. Uncle Tomás insisted that I keep a notebook, and every Saturday I recorded how many frames were full, whether the queen had been seen and what the weather was like. At first I found this tedious. Then, one morning in May, my notes showed that the colony had stopped growing, and it was my record, not his memory, that helped us work out why. The queen was old, and the workers were quietly preparing to replace her. \"See?\" he said, with a smile. \"The notebook noticed before we did.\"\n\nThe stings were the part I had worried about most. I was stung eleven times that summer, and I can report that it is never painless, although the pain fades faster than you would expect. What surprised me more was how quickly I stopped flinching. Uncle Tomás taught me to move slowly, breathe evenly and use as little smoke as possible, and I noticed that the calmer I was, the calmer the bees seemed to be.\n\nBy June, he had decided to take a back seat. He still came up to the roof every Saturday, but he sat in the shade with a flask of coffee while I opened the hives, inspected the frames and made the decisions. I made mistakes, of course. Once I left a lid slightly open before a rainstorm, and I spent a miserable afternoon with a towel and a bucket. He said nothing at the time. The following week, though, he quietly bought me a better lid.\n\nNot everyone shared our enthusiasm. Mrs Ibáñez on the fourth floor wrote a letter to the building committee saying that bees were dangerous and should be removed. Uncle Tomás did not argue. Instead, he invited her up to the roof, gave her a veil and let her watch. The hives are out of sight from the street, so most residents had no idea they were there; once she had seen them for herself, she said, she understood why we bothered. Within a month she was telling other neighbours to mind their own business when they complained about the buzzing.\n\nIn September we harvested our first honey: eleven kilos, dark gold and tasting faintly of the lime trees along the avenue. Selling a few jars paid for new equipment, but that was never the point. What I really took from the summer was a different way of looking at the city. I used to see it as a place made of concrete, noise and traffic. Now I see it as a landscape that thousands of small creatures cross every day, and I find I walk more slowly beneath the trees.",
