    "text": "At a quarter past two on Saturday mornings, my alarm goes off, and for about ten seconds I seriously consider throwing it out of the window. Then I remember the smell. By the time I reach Panadería Santos, ten minutes' walk from my house through streets so empty that I can hear my own footsteps, the whole corner smells of warm bread, and my bad mood has vanished. It is the best part of the job, and nobody warned me about it.\n\nI got the job in March, after my cousin Rafa left for university and created a gap in Mr Santos's weekend team. I had never worked before, and I had certainly never been awake at that hour except on the night of a long flight. Mr Santos is a short, round man with flour permanently on his glasses, and he loves to talk. He asks about my family, my marks at school, my opinions on football. But when the clock above the ovens strikes three, the conversation stops in mid-sentence. \"Right,\" he says. \"Let's get down to business.\" From then on, nobody speaks unless it is necessary.\n\nThe work is harder than I expected. Dough has to be weighed, shaped, left to rise and shaped again, and every stage has its own timing. My first month was mostly a matter of carrying heavy trays and trying not to get in anyone's way. In my fifth week, however, I was trusted with the ovens, and I promptly forgot to set the timer on the second batch of rolls. I was chatting to a quiet baker called Gustavo when he sniffed the air and looked at me. We reached the oven in the nick of time: the rolls were a shade darker than they should have been, but they were saved. Mr Santos took one look at them and laughed. \"Everyone burns something,\" he said. \"The trick is to do it only once.\"\n\nMr Santos has run the bakery for thirty-one years, and for most of that time he has done things his own way. Lately, though, his daughter Inés has been trying to bring the business up to date. She has designed new paper bags with a simple wheat-ear logo, and she has opened an online account where she posts photographs of the first loaves out of the oven. Mr Santos is sceptical. \"Bread doesn't need a brand identity,\" he told me, shaking his head. \"People can smell us from the corner.\" Yet I noticed that he was the first to reach for a bag when a customer asked for a photograph.\n\nThe recipes, on the other hand, are no longer set in stone. One Saturday I suggested adding a handful of seeds to the plain rolls, because my friends complained that they were boring. I expected to be ignored. Instead, Mr Santos made a trial batch the following week, tasted it in silence and wrote \"seed rolls\" on the board. They now sell out by eight o'clock. Students from the school next door are our most loyal customers; they want the most bang for their buck, and a warm cheese roll costs less than a bus ticket.\n\nBy nine o'clock the shelves are empty, the floors are swept and the first proper queue of the day is forming outside. This is when we call it a day, although for everybody else the day is only beginning. I walk home in the thin early sunlight, tired in a way that feels quite different from being tired after school. My hands smell of yeast, my feet ache, and I have earned a little money. I have also learnt something that no exam has taught me: that there is real satisfaction in finishing a day's work before most people have woken up to start theirs.",
