    "text": "Every school morning, millions of teenagers drag themselves out of bed, stumble to a bus stop and spend the first lesson of the day staring blankly at a whiteboard. Parents call it laziness. Increasingly, scientists call it biology.\n\nResearch from several countries suggests that during adolescence the body's internal clock shifts. Teenagers begin to produce the hormone that makes us sleepy later in the evening than children or adults do, which means that falling asleep at ten o'clock is genuinely difficult for many of them. When the alarm goes off at half past six, a teenager may have had only six hours of sleep, whereas most experts recommend eight to ten. The result, according to the researchers, is not simply yawning in class but poorer concentration, worse moods and, over time, a greater risk of health problems.\n\nSome schools have decided to act. One secondary school in the north of England moved its first lesson from eight o'clock to nine for a year as an experiment. Teachers reported that fewer students arrived late and that the first lesson of the day, once the quietest of the timetable, became one of the liveliest. Attendance improved slightly, and the school nurse noticed a drop in visits from students complaining of headaches. The deciding factor for the head teacher, though, was not the figures but a simple observation: \"For the first time in years, the students looked awake.\"\n\nNot everyone is convinced. Changing a school's hours affects far more than the students. Bus companies plan their routes around existing times, and many parents work shifts that begin early and cannot easily be rearranged. Sports teams worry that training will be squeezed into the dark winter evenings, and teachers with young children of their own wonder how they will manage the school run. The debate has sometimes degenerated into a game of pass the buck: schools blame the transport companies, the companies blame the local council, and the council points out that the timetable is the school's decision.\n\nMedia coverage has not helped. Headlines tend to announce that \"Later starts boost exam results by 20 per cent\" or, equally confidently, that \"Experiment fails to work\". In reality the evidence is mixed. Sleep improves reliably; marks improve in some studies and barely change in others. Researchers point out that a school day is shaped by many things, from the quality of teaching to the provision of a decent breakfast, and that it is hard to isolate the effect of a single hour on the clock.\n\nWhat the studies do agree on is that the traditional timetable is not set in stone. It was designed in an age when most children walked to school and many adults worked in factories, and nobody ever tested whether it suited teenagers' bodies. A growing number of schools now treat their hours as something to be tested and updated, much like a syllabus, rather than as a tradition to be defended.\n\nNone of this means that every school should simply push everything back by an hour. Rural schools that depend on a single bus, or families who cannot afford extra childcare, face real difficulties. But the question is worth asking, and it is better answered with trials and data than with slogans. After all, the students who sit through those first lessons have been asking it for years, usually in a very quiet voice.",
