    "text": "Daniel had never wanted to come on the school hiking trip. His mother had talked him into it, promising that fresh air would do him good, and by eleven o'clock on the first morning he was ready to admit that she might be right. The path climbed gently through pine woods, the sky was a hard, bright blue and there was nothing to do but walk. Beside him, Camila, who was never quiet for more than a minute, was explaining why her phone was better than his.\n\nAt noon the group stopped for lunch beside a stream. Mr Ferreira, the geography teacher, counted heads, reminded them to follow the red markers on the trees and sent them off in pairs for the afternoon stage. \"Stay together, and keep to the path,\" he said. \"If you're unsure, stop and wait.\" Daniel promised he would. Twenty minutes later he and Camila were arguing about a fork in the track. The markers had disappeared, Camila was certain the left path was the right one, and Daniel, who had never won an argument with her, gave in.\n\nIt took an hour to realise their mistake. The woods had thinned, the path had narrowed to a line of loose stones and the only sound was the wind. Daniel pulled out his phone; there was no signal, and the battery showed fourteen per cent. \"We should go back,\" he said. Camila, for once, did not argue. But when they turned round, every tree looked exactly like the one before it, and the stream they had followed seemed to have vanished, out of sight behind a ridge they did not remember climbing.\n\nBy four o'clock the temperature was dropping. Daniel tried not to think about the weather forecast, which had mentioned rain in the evening. Camila was limping; she had twisted her ankle on a loose stone, and although she insisted it was nothing, she had gone very pale. Daniel took off his scarf and used it to tie up her ankle as tightly as he dared, remembering a lesson from a first-aid course that he had thought pointless at the time. \"Better?\" he asked. \"Slightly,\" she said, with an attempt at a smile. \"Don't tell anyone I said so.\"\n\nThey decided to call it a day and shelter beneath an overhanging rock, where the ground was at least dry. Neither of them spoke for a long time. Daniel found himself thinking about his mother, about the absurd argument at the fork and about the one thing he had been asked to do: stay on the path. Then Camila reached into her bag and produced a whistle on a bright orange cord. \"Mr Ferreira gave us one each,\" she said. \"You didn't read the leaflet, did you?\" He hadn't. He blew three long blasts, as the leaflet had presumably explained, and then they waited.\n\nNothing happened for twenty minutes. The first drops of rain were falling when they heard a faint shout, then another, closer. A torch swung between the trees, and Mr Ferreira appeared, breathless and soaked, with two parents behind him. They had heard the whistle from the valley and had arrived in the nick of time: another half-hour and the path back would have been in darkness and mud. Daniel was at a loss for words. He had prepared a long apology while he waited, but all he could manage was a thin \"Sorry\". \"Next time,\" said Mr Ferreira, handing round dry jackets, \"read the leaflet.\"\n\nOn the bus home, Camila fell asleep against the window with her ankle propped on a rucksack. He still had no wish to go on another hike. But he noticed, with some surprise, that he was already thinking about how he would describe it all to his mother, and that he would not leave out the part about the whistle.",
