    "text": "The email arrived on a Tuesday evening: our team had been accepted into the regional robotics final, three weeks away. We had expected to feel delighted. In reality, the four of us stared at each other across the lab table, because we all knew the same uncomfortable fact. Our robot could follow a line on the floor, pick up a plastic cube and drop it in a box, but only if nothing went wrong, and something always went wrong.\n\nOur teacher, Ms Valdez, had warned us that most teams fail not because their robots are bad, but because their organisation is. She was right. Our first meeting after the email was chaos: Iván wanted to rebuild the arm, Sofía wanted to change the sensors and I wanted to rewrite the program from scratch. After forty minutes of shouting, Ms Valdez, who had been marking exercise books in the corner, put down her pen and told us to choose one problem. Just one.\n\nWe chose the sensors. They were reading the floor differently depending on how bright the room was, which explained why the robot behaved perfectly in the lab and wandered off the line in the school hall. Sofía spent a week testing positions, and I wrote a short program that measured the light at the start of each run and adjusted for it. The code was tidy, but the documentation was not, and I discovered how important that was when I came back to it a week later and could not remember what half of the lines were for. After that, we made a rule: nothing was finished until it was explained in writing.\n\nThe competition itself took place in a sports centre in the middle of a storm. Rain drummed on the roof, and at one point a power cut stopped the whole event for twenty minutes. This year the final offered two modalities, line-following and free navigation, and we had entered the first. Teams around us had robots like small tanks, with cameras, claws and shining metal. Ours was made of aluminium, cardboard and what Iván called \"optimism\". We were terrified.\n\nOur first run was a disaster. Just before it, a judge announced that the organisers had issued an update to the rules: robots now had to complete the course in ninety seconds instead of two minutes. Everyone else seemed to have known about this for a week. We hadn't checked the website. I felt my stomach drop, and I was sure we had missed the boat. The robot took the first corner perfectly, then turned in a slow circle, as if unable to decide where to go, and the clock ran out with the cube still on the floor.\n\nWe had forty minutes until the second run, and nobody spoke. Then Sofía, who had been staring at the table, said quietly, \"We don't need to be perfect. We need to be faster.\" In the time it takes to drink a bottle of water, we raised the speed of the motors, shortened the path to the box and changed three lines of the program, noting each change as we went. Ms Valdez, to her credit, said nothing. She simply held an umbrella over our laptop.\n\nThe second run lasted seventy-one seconds. The robot sped round the corner, grabbed the cube at the first attempt and dropped it into the box with nineteen seconds to spare. Nobody had believed we could pull it off, least of all us. We finished fifth out of twelve, which is not a trophy, but I have never been so proud of a piece of cardboard.\n\nWalking out through the rain afterwards, I thought about what I had learnt. You cannot plan for everything. And the best teams are not necessarily the ones with the best robots, but the ones who keep talking when things go wrong.",
