    "text": "When my father announced that we were selling the flat and moving onto a narrowboat, my mother laughed for a full minute. Then she saw his face and stopped. Dad had fallen head over heels in love with a rusty green boat called Heron that he had seen for sale beside a canal on his way home from work, and from that moment there was no changing his mind. Within three months, I had gone from a bedroom with a view of a petrol station to a cabin as wide as a wardrobe.\n\nI had expected to hate it. My friends certainly thought I would. \"You'll be living in a tin can,\" said Lena, who has never in her life been in anything smaller than a double garage. And at first she seemed right: there was nowhere to put anything, I hit my head every morning on the same low beam, and the toilet needed emptying every four days, an activity I will not describe. People on the towpath stared at us as they walked past with their dogs, and I made up my mind to stay inside.\n\nSlowly, though, the boat began to teach me things. A boat is not a flat; you cannot simply do your own thing without thinking about everybody else. Everything has a place, because otherwise it ends up on the floor, or in the canal. Mum, who used to say she could not live without a dishwasher, discovered that washing up with a view of ducks was far less terrible than she had feared. Dad, who had never fixed anything before, became an expert in engines, with the help of an enormous manual and a retired neighbour called Mr Okafor, who knew exactly when to offer advice and when to mind his own business.\n\nThe boat is also unexpectedly social. Boaters nod to each other and tie up side by side at night; strangers stop to talk about the weather, the water level or the best place for a pub lunch. I used to think that people who lived on boats were a bit odd. I had judged a book by its cover, I suppose. The people we have met include a nurse, a violin teacher, a retired pilot and a family of five who run a floating bookshop.\n\nOf course, there are moments when I miss ordinary life. Last winter the canal froze, and for nine days we could not move; the heating broke on the third, and I did my homework in a sleeping bag. Another time, a boat passed too quickly and our mugs flew off the table. And when the mist comes down in the evening and the other boats vanish out of sight, I sometimes feel as if we are the only people left in the world. Most of the time that feels peaceful. Occasionally it feels lonely.\n\nThis spring Dad finally took us through a lock without hitting anything. It sounds a small achievement, but if you have ever stood in a stone box while the water rises around you and a ten-tonne boat swings gently towards the gate, you will know it is not. He looked at me with such delight that I forgot to be embarrassed. \"We did that,\" he said. And I realised that I had said \"we\", too.\n\nI am not sure I would choose boat life for ever. I want to go to university, and universities are not often found beside canals. But ask me whether I would swap Heron for the flat above the petrol station, and the answer is no.",
