    "text": "Most sixteen-year-olds count themselves lucky if they have passed their driving theory test. Elena Marchetti, however, has already flown alone more than sixty times, in an aircraft with no engine. [[1]] \"Everybody says it must be terrifying,\" she laughs, \"but honestly the scariest part is the thirty seconds before the winch pulls you into the air.\"\n\nIt all began at an air show, where her grandfather, a retired engineer, took her one summer afternoon. [[2]] \"I was nine, and I wanted to go up there immediately,\" she says. \"He had to talk me into waiting until I was old enough.\"\n\nAt fourteen she finally joined the local gliding club, and the first months were less glamorous than she had imagined. [[3]] Gliders are heavy to move, and every flight needs a team of people to launch it. \"You don't just turn up and fly,\" she explains.\n\nGliding has a reputation for being expensive, and in some countries it is. But clubs run mostly by volunteers keep the costs lower than most people expect, and Elena pays for her flights with the money she earns on Saturdays at a garden centre. She also helps to run the club's youth programme, which has doubled in size in two years, partly because of the photographs she posts online.\n\nLearning to fly without an engine means learning to read the sky. [[4]] Pilots look for thermals, columns of warm air that rise from sun-heated ground, and circle inside them to gain height. \"On a good day you can pull off a flight of three hours without a single drop of fuel,\" Elena says.\n\nSafety, she insists, is the part of the sport that is never negotiable. [[5]] Instructors inspect every aircraft before the first launch, and pilots run through a written checklist even when they have done it hundreds of times. Elena admits that the routine can feel repetitive. \"But that's the point,\" she says. \"The day you stop checking is the day something goes wrong.\"\n\nHer first solo flight lasted just seven minutes. [[6]] \"I was at a loss for words afterwards,\" she remembers. \"My instructor asked me how it was, and all I could say was 'Quiet.' Completely, wonderfully quiet.\"\n\nElena now hopes to study aerospace engineering and, one day, to compete in the national championships. For the moment, though, she is content to spend her weekends on the airfield, waiting for the wind to be right.",
    "bank": {
     "A": "Among the displays, one aircraft held her attention more than all the others: a silent glider tracing slow circles above the field.",
     "B": "Yet when she landed, she was shaking with a mixture of nerves and sheer delight.",
     "C": "A glider is always sinking slowly through the air, so the pilot's task is to find air that is rising faster.",
     "D": "Her classmates, understandably, tend to react with a mixture of envy and disbelief.",
     "E": "Not having an engine, she points out, also makes each flight far cheaper than most people imagine.",
     "F": "Most of her Sundays were spent pushing aircraft across the grass, filling in logbooks and making endless cups of tea.",
     "G": "Gliders may have no engine to fail, but there are still plenty of things that can go wrong."
    },
