    "intro": "You are going to read about five young people who describe a hobby they took up during the holidays. For questions 1-10, choose from the people (A-E). The people may be chosen more than once.",
    "stem": "Which young person…",
    "sections": [
     {
      "l": "A",
      "h": "Marco, 16 — birdwatching",
      "t": "Before this summer I couldn't have told you the difference between a sparrow and a finch. Then my grandfather lent me his old binoculars, and I decided to read up on the birds of our valley before I went out with them. That turned out to be a smart move, because I could suddenly recognise things I had walked past all my life. Now I get up at five most mornings and sit completely still beside the river for hours. I write down everything I see, and my records go to a scientific research project at the university that follows how bird numbers change from year to year. The most important point I've learnt is patience: the birds simply won't hurry for you. It is strange to think that my notebook might help real scientists."
     },
     {
      "l": "B",
      "h": "Elena, 15 — video comedy",
      "t": "My cousin showed me how to use a video-editing app in July, and since then I've made eleven short films in my bedroom. Most of them send up the presenters of the cooking programmes my mum watches, with me wearing a tea towel on my head and shouting about butter. One of them got over a thousand views, which still amazes me. The only problem is at home. Filming in the kitchen has become a bone of contention, because my parents say I take over the whole house and leave cables everywhere. We argue about it most evenings, though last week my dad admitted that the tea-towel video had made him laugh so much he cried. I think that may count as progress."
     },
     {
      "l": "C",
      "h": "Priya, 16 — bicycle repair",
      "t": "I joined a charity workshop in the first week of the holidays because a friend dared me. The volunteers mend old bicycles, which are then shipped to schools in poorer countries, where children sometimes walk ten kilometres every morning. On my first day I couldn't even take off a wheel. Luckily the organisers split up the volunteers into small teams, so one group strips the bikes, another cleans them and a third tests the brakes. I was put in the cleaning team, and by August I was allowed to repair brakes. The workshop belongs to a retired shopkeeper who sold up his bike shop last year and now spends all his time here. I firmly believe that nothing makes you feel more useful than fixing something that someone will really use."
     },
     {
      "l": "D",
      "h": "Jack, 17 — climbing",
      "t": "I had never been higher than the third floor of a building before a friend took me to a climbing wall in July. My heart was racing, and I had such strong feelings of panic that I couldn't make my hands let go of the wall. Honestly, I nearly gave up. We almost fell out when I refused to try the harder route. But after a week or two the fear began to settle down, and now I look forward to every session. What I've learnt is that climbing mostly comes down to trusting the person holding your rope, because if they are not paying attention, nothing else matters. In September we're going to try a real rock face outdoors, and I'm both terrified and excited."
     },
     {
      "l": "E",
      "h": "Sofia, 14 — baking",
      "t": "I started baking in the holidays because I was bored, and it quickly became more serious than I expected. My first loaves were so hard that my brother used them as door stoppers! Then a neighbour hit the nail on the head when she said I should practise one recipe until I got it right, and after that my cakes improved. In August I held a small sale outside our house, and I designed my own advertising campaign for it: posters on every lamp-post in the street. Fifty people came, and everything sold out by lunchtime. I'd love to turn it into a career one day and open my own bakery, although my mum says I should finish school first."
     }
    ],
