    "intro": "You are going to read an article in which five teenagers describe a cycling trip. For questions 1-10, choose from the people (A-E). The people may be chosen more than once.",
    "stem": "Which cyclist",
    "sections": [
     {
      "l": "A",
      "h": "Jonas: beside the Danube",
      "t": "Last summer I cycled for eleven days beside the Danube with two friends. The route is almost completely flat, so we did not need to be athletes, and the path is so well signposted that I used the map only twice. The trouble was the heat. By midday it was too hot to enjoy anything, and we ended up getting up at five to cover most of the distance before lunch. We also underestimated how much we would eat: small villages shut their shops in the afternoon, and more than once we went to bed hungry. Still, I would go again, because arriving in a different country every few days made a very ordinary bike trip feel like a proper adventure."
     },
     {
      "l": "B",
      "h": "Marta: the Scottish islands",
      "t": "I am not a natural cyclist, which is why a week on a chain of Scottish islands was probably not the wisest choice. The scenery is breathtaking, and the roads are so quiet that I once rode for an hour without seeing a single car, and the sea is rarely out of sight. However, the wind was a constant enemy, and slopes that look small on the map take forever when you are pedalling into a gale. What saved the trip was the people. Every night we stayed in a family-run guest house, and each host seemed to know exactly how tired we were and how much porridge we needed. I would advise anyone planning this route to book the ferries well ahead; we missed one by five minutes and ended up sleeping in a waiting room."
     },
     {
      "l": "C",
      "h": "Sven: over the mountain pass",
      "t": "I took my bike over an Alpine pass with my older brother, who is a keen racer. I was the slow one, and I will admit that there were moments on the climb when I wanted to throw the bike into a ditch. We got there, though, and the descent, fifteen kilometres of smooth road without pedalling, was the best twenty minutes of my life. Equipment matters, too: my borrowed bike had the wrong gears, and every kilometre was harder than it should have been. What I would tell other beginners is to ignore the numbers on your brother's computer and ride at your own pace. Nothing about a route like this is set in stone, so shorten the day if you need to."
     },
     {
      "l": "D",
      "h": "Chloe: across Wales for charity",
      "t": "Our youth club raised money for a children's hospital by cycling across the country in six days. It was the first time I had ridden with a group, and it changed how I think about exercise: we sang, told terrible jokes and shared out the snacks like a family. The sponsorship gave the whole thing a purpose. When I wanted to stop on the fourth day, I thought about all the people who had given money, and somehow my legs kept moving. Only one thing spoiled it: nine punctures among twelve riders, which meant the support van was busier than we had planned. If I did it again, I would learn to mend a tyre myself instead of waiting for help."
     },
     {
      "l": "E",
      "h": "Hassan: a short trip from home",
      "t": "Everyone assumes you need two weeks, but our best trip lasted three days. We rode from our front door to a campsite beside a lake only sixty kilometres away, ate sandwiches, swam and rode back. No flights, no ferries, no expensive equipment. My advice is to look for adventures near home. We discovered a ruined castle fifteen minutes from our house that nobody in my family had ever visited. The main drawback was the weather: we had one dry afternoon in three days and spent the first night in a damp tent. But it cost almost nothing, and I would happily do it every month. My parents tried to talk me into joining an organised tour next year, but I would rather do my own thing."
     }
    ],
