    "intro": "You are going to read an article in which five young people describe learning something new. For questions 1-10, choose from the people (A-E). The people may be chosen more than once.",
    "stem": "Which person",
    "sections": [
     {
      "l": "A",
      "h": "Nadia: the piano",
      "t": "I started piano because my grandmother talked me into it. She promised to pay me a euro for every hour I practised, which seemed like a good deal until I realised how many hours it takes before you can play anything recognisable. My first six months were spent on scales, and I almost gave up at Christmas. What changed everything was a duet with my cousin. Playing together made practice feel like a game instead of a punishment, and the notes started to make sense. My mother says the house has never been so noisy, and she means it kindly. I now practise because I want to, not for the euros, although I do still collect them. Next month I am playing at a family wedding, which is terrifying."
     },
     {
      "l": "B",
      "h": "Luis: Japanese",
      "t": "I wanted to read comics without waiting for translations, so I began teaching myself Japanese with a free app. Updates arrive every week with new vocabulary, and the daily reminders are annoying but effective. The writing was the hardest part: three different systems! I made hundreds of mistakes, and once told a Japanese penfriend that I was a cat. She laughed, corrected me kindly and we have written to each other ever since. I am still far from reading a whole volume, but I can follow the plot of a short story, which feels like a small miracle. My advice to anybody starting a language is to speak to real people as early as possible, however badly, because mistakes are the quickest teachers there are."
     },
     {
      "l": "C",
      "h": "Dara: skateboarding",
      "t": "Nobody teaches you to skate; you fall until you stop falling. For the first month my knees and elbows were permanently bruised, and my mother nearly banned the whole thing. Learning the ropes was far from painless, but strangely I never wanted to quit, because every tiny improvement, staying on for five seconds or turning without falling, felt huge. The skate park has an unwritten rule that older kids help beginners, and that is how I met most of my friends. Parents think it is a dangerous hobby, and perhaps it is, but I have never felt so free as when everything finally works and the board seems to be part of my feet. I would not be the person I am without that ugly, bruised first month."
     },
     {
      "l": "D",
      "h": "Tomasz: chess",
      "t": "My school chess club is famous for being quiet, but I joined because my brother always beat me and I wanted revenge. For a year I lost nearly every game. Then my coach told me to stop playing and start looking at my lost games. Working out why I had lost taught me more than winning ever had. Last month I finally beat my brother in front of the whole family. He blamed the lamp. I still lose often, but now I know exactly why. People imagine that chess is only for geniuses, but in my club the best player is a boy who simply never gets tired of studying, and who explains his moves to anyone who asks."
     },
     {
      "l": "E",
      "h": "Amira: cooking",
      "t": "I had never cooked more than toast when my parents started working late and somebody had to feed the family. I began with videos online, but the cooks on screen make everything look easy, and my first risotto was a grey disaster. Eventually I stopped following recipes to the letter and started to do my own thing, adding a little more garlic here, less salt there. My younger sister says my pasta is the best in the street. The greatest lesson was patience: good food cannot be hurried. These days I plan the week's meals on Sunday, shop on Monday and cook for about an hour every evening, and I would not give it up for anything."
     }
    ],
