    "intro": "You are going to read an article in which four students describe the clubs they belong to. For questions 1-10, choose from the clubs (A-D). The clubs may be chosen more than once.",
    "stem": "Which club member",
    "sections": [
     {
      "l": "A",
      "h": "Sofía: the debate club",
      "t": "I joined debate because I could not stand being quiet in class discussions. At first I used to take a back seat, nodding while the others argued. Then our coach, Mr Okoye, insisted that everyone must speak in every session, even if only for thirty seconds. We are given a side of the argument by lot, so I often have to defend a view I disagree with. That was uncomfortable at first, but I now understand my opponents far better than I used to. Last spring we pulled off an unexpected victory against a school with three times as many members. I still cannot quite believe it. What I like most is that nobody judges you for being nervous; everybody remembers how frightening the first speech was, and the older members always clap loudest for the newcomers."
     },
     {
      "l": "B",
      "h": "Kenji: the photography club",
      "t": "Our photography club has only eight members but a whole cupboard of old film cameras. People expect digital, but film makes you slow down: you have twelve shots, so every one matters. We develop the pictures ourselves in a tiny darkroom, which smells awful and which I love. The first ten minutes of each meeting are pure chatter, and then we get down to business. The club's weakness is organisation. We have no proper documentation of who borrowed which camera, so lenses regularly vanish. Our teacher does not mind; he says that if you want to learn the ropes of photography, you should lose a few lenses. Another thing I love is the exhibition at the end of the year, when our best pictures hang in the school corridor and even students who never talk to us stop to look at them for a long time."
     },
     {
      "l": "C",
      "h": "Lara: the gardening club",
      "t": "Gardening club has no entry exam and no experience is needed. We look after a small plot behind the gym where we grow tomatoes, beans and, in a good year, pumpkins. You get real bang for your buck: the club costs nothing and you take vegetables home. It is also oddly calming, because nobody checks their phone while their hands are in the soil. The downside is the weather: in winter nothing happens, so we meet less. Our biggest triumph was selling pumpkins at the school fair, which paid for a whole set of new tools. We also have a compost heap that is the pride of the club, and a rule that anybody who forgets to water the plants on a Friday has to bring in biscuits on Monday. Everyone is very careful about the plants."
     },
     {
      "l": "D",
      "h": "Hana: the student newspaper",
      "t": "We publish every month, so there are deadlines, arguments about headlines and a lot of coffee. Nothing is set in stone until the editor signs it off, and she changes her mind about things every day. Our biggest problem is checking facts: everybody says it is someone else's job, and we pass the buck until the last minute. I joined as a proofreader and now write a column. Our best story was an investigation into the prices in the school canteen. It did not win a prize, but the head teacher read it and lowered the price of fruit, which I count as a victory. The best part of the job, though, is the Thursday meeting, when thirty people with thirty different opinions crowd into a tiny room and somehow decide the front page together before the caretaker locks the door."
     }
    ],
